Installing imud
===============

From the apt repository (Raspberry Pi OS / Debian, arm64/armhf — recommended)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    curl -fsSL https://richcreations.github.io/imud/apt/KEY.gpg \
      | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/imud.gpg

    printf 'Types: deb\nURIs: %s\nSuites: %s\nComponents: main\nSigned-By: %s\n' \
      'https://richcreations.github.io/imud/apt' \
      "$(. /etc/os-release && echo "$VERSION_CODENAME")" \
      '/usr/share/keyrings/imud.gpg' \
      | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/imud.sources >/dev/null

    sudo apt update && sudo apt install imud imud-wmm-data

The suite is read from /etc/os-release, so the above is identical on bookworm
and trixie. If you added /etc/apt/sources.list.d/imud.list under earlier
instructions, remove it so apt does not see the repository twice. Optional
packages: imud-utils (imud-mon), and the bridges imud-signalk / imud-mqtt /
imud-influxdb / imud-mavlink / imud-prometheus. Repository landing page:
https://richcreations.github.io/imud/apt/


From source
-----------

Prerequisites (Raspberry Pi OS / Debian trixie, Bookworm, or similar):

    sudo apt install build-essential libgpiod-dev

Build and install the core — the imud daemon plus imud-cal, imud-status,
the client libraries, the config, the service unit, and man pages — and the
World Magnetic Model data (needed for true-heading output):

    make
    sudo make install
    sudo make install-wmm-data

There is an optional ./configure step. On Linux `make` alone detects
everything it has to, so it is never required; run it to see what this host
can build before building, and to record the answers:

    ./configure                  # writes config.mk, prints what is and is not here
    ./configure --help           # the options
    make distclean               # remove config.mk and start over

It fails only on what the daemon itself needs — a C11 compiler, pthreads and
libm. The i2c-dev and spidev headers are not among them: without them the
build takes the null bus backend, which still runs driver = sim end to end,
and --with-linux-bus is how you demand them instead. Everything else is
reported, not required: host byte order (the wire format is little-endian
either way), a missing libmosquitto costs the MQTT bridge, a missing pandoc
costs `make docs-texi`.

Installs to /usr/local by default. Override the prefix, or stage into a
packaging root, with the standard variables — or with the matching configure
option, which writes them into config.mk instead:

    sudo make install PREFIX=/usr
    make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage PREFIX=/usr        # staged, no root needed
    ./configure --prefix=/usr --libdir=/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu

The prefix reaches the binaries, not just the file copy: PREFIX, ETCDIR,
DATADIR, RUNDIR and STATEDIR are compiled in, so an imud built for a prefix
finds its own config, calibration, sockets and WMM data. A package manager
that defines the service itself, and refuses writes outside its prefix, wants
--with-service=none — no unit, no state directory:

    ./configure --prefix=$HOME/.local --etcdir=$HOME/.config/imud \
                --rundir=$HOME/.local/var/run \
                --statedir=$HOME/.local/var/imud --with-service=none

Users and groups: `make install` creates the system user imud, whose primary
group imud owns /run/imud and both AF_UNIX sockets. Hardware access is by the
supplementary groups gpio, i2c and spi, which are CREATED if absent —
Raspberry Pi OS ships them, stock Debian does not, and imud.service will not
start without them. The installed udev rule 60-imud.rules grants those groups
the /dev/i2c-*, /dev/spidev* and /dev/gpiochip* nodes, which is what Raspberry
Pi OS provides in its own 99-com.rules. To read the stream socket or run
imud-status as an ordinary user, join the imud group:
sudo adduser "$USER" imud.

Packaging notes: when DESTDIR is set the install is a pure file copy — no
system user is created, no groups are made, and systemctl/udevadm/ldconfig are
never invoked (those belong to a package's maintainer scripts). Packagers
should also set SVCDIR=/lib/systemd/system (service units are generated from
etc/*.service.in, or etc/*.plist.in on a launchd host, with the chosen bin and
config dirs) and UDEVDIR=/usr/lib/udev/rules.d
(the default /etc/udev/rules.d is right for a source install but is reserved
for the admin in a package; udev never reads rules from PREFIX). CC/CFLAGS/
CPPFLAGS/LDFLAGS from the environment (e.g. dpkg-buildflags) are respected.
`make dist` produces the upstream release tarball from git HEAD. See
packaging/README.

Client development: `make install` ships libimud (libimud.so.0 + imud.h +
pkg-config metadata). Third-party apps build with

    cc app.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libimud)

and stay binary-compatible across imud upgrades (see man 3 libimud). The
bridges link libimud themselves; when running them from the build tree on
Linux (not installed), prefix LD_LIBRARY_PATH=. so they find the freshly
built library.

Optional bridge daemons are built and installed separately — each is its own
package with its own dependencies, config, and docs:

    make bridges                                       # build all bridges
    sudo make install-signalk                          # Signal K bridge
    sudo make install-mqtt                             # MQTT bridge (needs libmosquitto-dev)
    sudo make install-influxdb                         # InfluxDB bridge
    sudo make install-mavlink                          # MAVLink bridge
    sudo make install-prometheus                       # Prometheus exporter
    sudo make install-utils                            # imud-mon monitor + imud-imutest

Prebuilt arm64 and armhf .debs for Debian/Raspberry Pi OS bookworm and trixie are
attached to GitHub releases (https://github.com/richcreations/imud/releases):
imud, imud-utils, libimud0, libimud-dev, the five bridges, and imud-wmm-data.

The armhf .debs are built on Raspbian, whose baseline is ARMv6, so they run on
every Raspberry Pi that boots a 32-bit userland -- the Pi 1 and Pi Zero/Zero W
included. A package built to Debian's own armhf baseline (ARMv7) installs on
those boards and then dies on an illegal instruction, because both ports report
the same "armhf" from dpkg --print-architecture.

Edit the configuration for your hardware, then enable the service:

    sudo nano /etc/imud/imud.conf
    sudo systemctl enable --now imud

Other targets:

    make test            # run the test suite (from the repo root)
    sudo make uninstall  # remove installed files (config in /etc/imud is kept)


On macOS
--------

The daemon, the tools and the bridges build and run on macOS 14 Sonoma through
macOS 26 Tahoe, and CI runs the whole test suite on both ends of that range and
on Intel. ./configure is REQUIRED on this host, not optional — it is what
selects the backends a Mac has, where a plain make reaches for the Linux ones
and fails on linux/i2c-dev.h:

    brew install mosquitto        # only for the MQTT bridge
    ./configure && make
    sudo make install
    sudo launchctl bootstrap system \
        /Library/LaunchDaemons/io.github.richcreations.imud.plist

configure detects launchd from uname and installs a launchd job instead of a
systemd unit, into /Library/LaunchDaemons; --with-service=systemd|launchd|none
overrides the choice and --svcdir the directory. The job runs as root, because
macOS has no useradd and none of the unit's confinement — see the comment at
the top of etc/imud.plist.in. There is no udev rule and no imud user here, and
no /run either: the AF_UNIX paths in the installed config are rewritten to
/var/run, which every boot provides.

Stop it with `sudo launchctl bootout system/io.github.richcreations.imud`. A
LaunchDaemon also appears in System Settings > General > Login Items &
Extensions, where it can be switched off — the first place to look if the
daemon stops starting for no reason the log explains.

A Mac has no I2C or SPI node and no GPIO chip, so a real sensor comes in over
an FT232H USB bridge (i2c_bus = "ftdi:", int_gpio = 0; see docs/manual.md
§5.2), and driver = sim runs the pipeline with no hardware at all. There is no
.deb; build from source.

The full installation, configuration, and calibration guide is docs/manual.md
(installed to /usr/share/doc/imud/manual.md).
